+# DOJ passenger-call production and the county emergency recordings
+
+November 9, 2026 Eastern · **TEL01–TEL04 / AUD016-A1 / TC05–TC06**. Three previously unacquired Commission archive PDFs supply FBI transmittal copies and an investigative record of two attempts to copy Somerset County emergency calls. A separate Cambria County entry identifies a cassette-copy enclosure. The original media, delivery receipts and technical comparison of the recordings remain open. [National Archives finding aid, Team 7 Boxes 12–13](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/finding-aid.pdf#page=105) · [Exact ZIP members, ranges and hashes](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## The June 17 production and the emergency-call media
+
+| Record | Observed fields |
+| --- | --- |
+| Packet 2, pp. 1–3, pp. 1–2 lines 4 and 14–19, p. 3 lines 14–16 | Two differently rendered copies of the FBI’s **June 17, 2004** letter list **9 packets** for Request **35-13** and describe a rolling production. The adjacent cover says **“SUPPLEMENTAL to PACKET #2”** and **“COMMISSION COPY.”** These are production and assembly fields. A receipt and packet-level manifest are needed to establish what arrived and when. |
+| Packet 2, pp. 4–5, stamps **000000043 A–B** | The p. 4 image and p. 5 continuation date read **06/11/2002**. The p. 4 text layer reads **06711/2002**. P. 4 lines 30–46 describe a CD and a diskette of call summaries provided on **May 23, 2002**. The CD represented a copy of Somerset County **DVD No. 60**, dated **September 11, 2001**, titled *The Crash*. The FBI memo says a **May 29** review found a copying malfunction and incomplete call coverage. It records obtaining a second copy on **June 4**. The affected calls, whether the second copy was complete, and which version investigators later reviewed remain open. |
+| Packet 2, pp. 6–7, stamps **000000043 C–D**, p. 6 lines 53–60, p. 7 lines 7–9 and 23–32 | The **June 20, 2002** document describes a **1-A** envelope with a cassette copy of two Cambria County 911 calls. It describes an airport report and a separate crash report, with the latter transferred to Somerset County. The source does not supply a carrier or routing join between the cassette, the receiving center’s DVD and any particular passenger call. |
+
+The three acquisition dates or events in the Somerset memo are distinct from the DVD’s recorded date, the June 11 memo date and the 2004 Commission production. The two FBI cover copies share the dated production language. Their different raster hashes identify different scan representations and supply no second independent dispatch. The Somerset and Cambria materials may overlap at the reported transfer, pending a recording or dispatch-identifier match. [Commission ZIP](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip) · [Earlier audio custody comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/5c2a489db46eb230900d41b05f1710497e636965/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-recording-custody-relays-2026-11-02/README.md)
+
+## Packet 4 device descriptions and Packet 8 coverage
+
+- **Packet 4, p. 1, line 14.** The image reads **REQUESTS #35-13**. The text layer reads **REQUESTS #35-1.** The earlier public-reader comparison reported the same split. The archive copy provides a separately pinned source image. [Earlier reader comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/commit/a6346faf2b093cd4f24193f87c7f69d805ac5b27)
+- **Packet 4, pp. 3–4, stamps 122–123.** P. 3 lines 9–18 describe leads **AT280** and **AT584**, calling the Flight 93 conversation one made **“via an airphone.”** P. 4 lines 30–35 report via a state-police account that a family recipient **“received a cell phone call.”** Preserve both device descriptions and the differing reported recipients. It remains untested whether the reports refer to the same call leg, different legs or the same underlying account retold. They cannot assign a service type to a native call record. [Packet member and verification fields](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+- **Packet 8, pp. 3–4.** The June 16 index expands to **59** labels from **594–652**, including the printed **639–642** row on p. 4. The prior reader screen had **50** exact text matches. The newly acquired 60-page PDF has **50** exact text matches in its 56 numbered body positions, and six further labels were read from the images. The page images at pp. 49–50 show **638** followed by **642**. The combined sequence contains **56** labels and leaves **639–641** absent from this PDF. The six text-extraction misses and the three absent numbered pages are distinct. The original packet, release history and reason for the absent labels remain unresolved. [Prior reader comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/commit/a6346faf2b093cd4f24193f87c7f69d805ac5b27) · [Exact PDF counts and image controls](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Remaining originals and next discriminating test
+
+- **TEL01–TEL02.** Seek the June 17 delivery or receipt and the versioned 35-13 packet manifest, including the actual 639–641 images or an item-specific release record. Keep this production separate from Request 14 and its **M-WFA** series. [Request 14 comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/5031c6dfa73e1f59491f259e00f168c37cb21788/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-usage-report-2026-10-27/README.md)
+- **TEL03–TEL04.** Seek DVD No. 60, the first and second copies, diskette summaries, the Cambria 1-A cassette, custody entries and any review logs. A matched inventory and synchronized playback would test which calls survive, whether a transferred call occurs in both systems, and whether the second copy repaired the observed gap. The operator/source events, office transfer records, carrier field meanings, September 20 usage-report inputs and zero-duration exhibit construction remain separate dependencies.
+
+Three public ZIP members were retrieved by bounded ranges and validated against the archive index. The text layer of **167 physical pages** was screened. **30** fixed phrase locators were verified, and selected dates, count fields, enclosure identifiers, device labels, index rows and footers were inspected visually. This was a comparison of archive copies. The source bodies remain preserved separately. [Verification scope](SOURCE-PINS.json)
